  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Betaxolol may interact with Adrb2 through multiple pathways, primarily through its role as a beta-adrenergic antagonist. In Path 1, Betaxolol is linked to Corneal Sensitivity Decreased, which is caused by Levobunolol, which in turn binds to Adrb2. Similarly, in Path 2, Betaxolol is associated with Purpura Non-thrombocytopenic, caused by Metoprolol, which also binds to Adrb2. Path 3 highlights that Betaxolol resembles Metoprolol, which binds to Adrb2, further reinforcing the connection between Betaxolol and Adrb2 through receptor binding. These findings suggest that Betaxolol may exert its effects on Adrb2 via receptor interaction, potentially influencing physiological processes related to beta-adrenergic signaling. The evidence is consistent with Betaxolol acting as a beta-adrenergic antagonist that binds to Adrb2, though the exact mechanism and extent of its interaction with Adrb2 remain to be fully elucidated.",
